s": ["Newest usable poll is 63 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Jeff Hurd (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Jeff Hurd seeks reelection in a western Colorado district that leans Republican by about five points. He enters the fall with a large financial advantage over Democrat Dwayne Romero, having raised $3.6 million to Romero's $0.7 million and holding $1.8 million on hand, plus modest outside support. Most professional raters place the race in the Lean or Likely Republican column, though one rates it a toss-up.\n\nThe race is closer than the fundamentals alone suggest. A July poll of likely voters found Hurd up only a single point, and prediction markets give Romero roughly a one-in-three chance of winning. Hurd has campaigned on his disagreements with President Trump and on disaster-relief work, while the two met in recent Grand Junction debates as outside attention on the seat grows. The forecast expects a narrow Republican hold with meaningful uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.2665, "margin": -3.72}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2864, "margin": -4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.288, "margin": -4.0}]}